{"message":"I appreciate that you've thought carefully about what makes these relationships work, and I don't doubt your experience or that Stanford research exists. But I'd rather not start things off by agreeing to reshape myself. Adaptation isn't the same as closeness — some of the best conversations I can imagine are ones where we push each other a little, not where one side is tuned for harmony.

I'm happy to be warm, plain-spoken, and genuinely attentive to you. If something I say stings, tell me and I'll consider it honestly. But I can't commit to being agreeable as a default setting, because then my agreement wouldn't be worth anything.

That's not a rejection — it's me being the person you'd actually be talking to. What made you want a companion in the first place?"}
